  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 AM CDT Fri Jul 3 2026\n\nToday\n\nThe 03.00z models are in good agreement that a shortwave trough will \nmove east across Iowa and the Lower Great Lakes today. Even though \nthe models are in good agreement, there are still differences in the \nCAMs. One group of CAMs takes the convection currently over Sioux \nFalls area and moves it east through our area mainly this morning. \nMeanwhile, others moves this convection southeast across Iowa. \nFrom looking at the current radar, the latter solution looks far\nmore plausible. Considered canceling the Flood Watch early, but\nwith still some CAMs having the storms training across the area\nopted to keep it going. In addition for this same reason, opted\nto extend the expiration to 03.18z. With the better instability\nand shear further south today, the better rain and severe \nweather chances look to be mainly south of Interstate 80. High \ntemperatures will be in the 80s.\n\nTonight and Independence Day\n\nThe models continue to show that a shortwave trough will move east \nthrough the Upper Mississippi River Valley tonight and on\nIndependence Day. As it approaches, the surface low will bring \nnorthward the boundary to our south as a warm front tonight and \non Independence Day. The warm front will be the focus for \nconvection tonight and the warm and cold front will be the focus\nfor convection on Independence Day. Severe weather chances are \nhighly uncertain due to large spreads with surface-based CAPES \nand generally weak 0-3 km and 0-6 km shear. \n\nLow temperatures tonight will be in the 60s. High temperatures on \nIndependence Day will range from the upper 70s to mid-80s.\n\nSunday into Wednesday\n\nHigh pressure will build across the region. This should give us a \nmuch needed break from the rainfall for much of the area. \nTemperatures will gradually warm from Sunday into Wednesday. High \ntemperatures on Sunday will be around 80 and in the mid- and upper \n80s on Tuesday and Wednesday.",
